{
  "name": "scatter_ossetian",
  "description": "Ossetian — an Iranian language of the central Caucasus (North Ossetia / South Ossetia), written in Cyrillic. The defining structural feature is the three-way stop contrast — voiceless, ejective, voiced — at 4 places (bilabial, dental, velar, uvular). The ejective row is a Caucasian areal feature; no other Iranian language carries it. Two nasals (m, n). Voiceless fricatives f, s, ʃ, x, h; voiced v, z, ʒ, ɣ. Affricates ts, tʃ (also ejective counterparts tsʼ, tʃʼ which collapse onto the voiceless-affricate row on this atlas). Lateral /l/, trill /r/. Inventory drawn from Abaev (1964) and Erschler (2009).",
